# Test: `update firewall domain-group <name>` resolves the group's names now,
#       programs the addresses into the group's nftables set, and records the
#       move from the old addresses to the new ones in the change log.
# Spec: spec-firewall-domain-group -- Wiring Test row 4, user story 1
# VALIDATES: the operator path. The command is typed at `ze cli` over SSH and
#            reaches the forwarded plugin command handler (updateDomainGroup,
#            internal/component/firewall/plugins/domain/command.go), which
#            resolves through the plugin-to-hub resolve RPC, writes the answer
#            into the cache, re-applies the tables, and appends one change
#            record per name and family.
# PREVENTS: an update that reports a count while the kernel set stays empty, and
#           a change log that records a resolution without saying what it
#           replaced.
#
# RUN GREEN on 2026-09-06, and observed RED first: with changeLog.append
# (internal/component/firewall/plugins/domain/changelog.go) returning before it
# writes, the five file assertions below fail and the two stdout ones still pass,
# which is what says the change log is what they read. The command that runs it
# is `./le functional plugin`; on a host without CAP_NET_ADMIN and
# CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE it is skipped by the option below, and the runner that
# executes it in the gate is `./le qemu netns-test suites plugin`.
#
# The fixture is domainGroupUpdate
# (internal/test/fixture/netfilter_fixture_domain_group.go). It drives the
# daemon over the real SSH `ze cli` path rather than over a plugin channel,
# which is what Wiring row 4 asks for: a `plugin { external ... }` block would
# hand it a channel to dispatch on and would change what the daemon under test
# is running. In order it:
#   1. Waits for the daemon, waits for the SSH port, and provisions client
#      credentials with `ze init`.
#   2. Serves DNS on 127.0.0.1:53 (the address `system name-server` names
#      below) from the stub's default zone, which answers web.example.test with
#      A 203.0.113.10 and AAAA 2001:db8::10 at TTL 0.
#   3. Runs `update firewall domain-group web | json` and prints the answer.
#   4. Reads the kernel back until the set holds 203.0.113.10, and prints
#      NOTHING while doing it.
#   5. Changes the stub's A answer for web.example.test to 203.0.113.11.
#   6. Runs `update firewall domain-group web | json` again and prints the
#      answer.
#   7. Prints the kernel read-back of the set, once, at the end.
# Step 4 is silent on purpose: this file asserts on the FINAL contents of the
# set, and an intermediate dump would put the replaced address into the same
# buffer (see the file-level note below). It is not decoration either -- without
# it the change asserted here would have nothing proven to replace.
#
# The term matching the group is declared in the config below, not committed
# later. A daemon START reads its config through OnConfigure alone and runs no
# verify (configure, internal/component/firewall/plugins/domain/domain.go), so
# the refusal AC-6 describes belongs to a commit and not to a boot. What a boot
# does instead is log the same message and hold the table back
# (warnUncachedGroups, and dropTablesMissingAProvidedSet in
# internal/component/firewall/registry.go), which is exactly the cold start this
# test's first update ends.
#
# ONE SCENARIO PER FILE. An expect=stdout: assertion is FILE-level: the runner
# checks it against the concatenated output of every command in the file
# (runner_exec.go, rec.ClientOutput), stdout and stderr together. So the
# withdrawal half of this feature lives in
# test/plugin/firewall-domain-group-clear.ci, which is the file that may say an
# address is absent.
#
# This test programs nftables and binds port 53, so it needs CAP_NET_ADMIN and
# CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE, not merely Linux.
option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin,net-bind
# Serialize against every other test that binds port 53, the two `ze-test dns`
# scenarios included. The port is NODE-WIDE and the address is fixed by the
# type of the `system name-server` leaf, so two stubs cannot coexist. One group
# covers the nftables contention too: the ruleset is node-wide and the set names
# are derived from the group name (domain_v4_<name>, setNames in sets.go), and
# every test that would collide on those is in this group already. A test
# carries ONE exclusive group (Record.ExclusiveGroup, record_parse.go), so this
# has to be the one that covers both.
option=exclusive:group=dns-stub-port-53
# error, not the default. dropTablesMissingAProvidedSet
# (internal/component/firewall/registry.go) logs `set=domain_v4_web` at WARN
# every time this table is held back, and it is held back at boot until the
# first update resolves the group. The daemon's stderr lands in the same buffer
# the assertions below read (runner_exec.go, teeDaemonStderr), so that line
# would satisfy the set-name assertion on its own and leave it proving nothing.
option=env:var=ze.log.firewall:value=error
# ze.config.dir is what puts the change log where expect=file can read it.
# changeLogPath() (changelog.go) joins DefaultConfigDir() with
# firewall-domain-group.dns.jsonl, and DefaultConfigDir honours this override
# (paths.go). Without it the log is written beside the binary and no assertion
# below can see it.
option=env:var=ze.config.dir:value=.
option=timeout:value=30s

stdin=ze-fw:terminator=EOF_CONF
system {
	# The resolver the hub builds reads NameServers[0] as its server
	# (newResolvers, cmd/ze/hub/main_system.go), so this is what points every
	# lookup at the fixture's stub instead of at the host's real DNS.
	name-server 127.0.0.1;
	dns {
		# An empty path stops Ze writing a resolv.conf. The container this test
		# runs in is shared, and the file is not what the resolver reads here.
		resolv-conf-path "";
	}
	# The account the fixture logs in as. The hash is of "testpass", and it is
	# the same credential test/firewall/firewall-cli-show.ci uses.
	authentication {
		user operator {
			password "$2a$04$UlwuiuH82Unfsq.XEMPGJeDkXwbm3KW.nvVaVXOd/JeFK8VjMjrQO";
		}
	}
}

firewall {
	backend nft;
	domain-group web {
		domain-names [ web.example.test ];
		ttl-floor 60;
	}
	table fw_dgu {
		family inet;
		chain input {
			type filter;
			hook input;
			priority 0;
			policy accept;
			# The rule that matches the group. Until the first update resolves
			# it, this whole table is held back rather than programmed, which is
			# what the fixture's silent read-back in step 4 waits for the end of.
			term allow-web {
				from { source-domain-group web; }
				then { accept; }
			}
		}
	}
}

environment {
	ssh {
		enabled true;
		server main {
			ip 127.0.0.1;
			# $PORT2 is the second of the two ports the runner leases to this
			# test (TestPortSpan, internal/test/runner/ports.go). A fixed port
			# would collide with a sibling in this suite, which shares one
			# network namespace.
			port $PORT2;
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-fw
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze-test fixture plugin/firewall-domain-group-update $PORT2:timeout=25s
expect=exit:code=0

# The addresses reach the kernel. The set is read back from nftables, not from
# the plugin's own memory, so a cache that filled while ApplyAll never ran fails
# here.
expect=stdout:contains=domain_v4_web
expect=stdout:contains=203.0.113.11

# The change log names the old addresses and the new ones. The first record is
# the arrival, with nothing behind it; the second is the move. These are exact
# JSON substrings: changeRecord is marshalled field by field in declaration
# order (changelog.go), and append() replaces a nil Old or New with an empty
# slice precisely so a reader never has to infer a direction from a missing
# field. A product that logged only the new value passes neither line.
#
# NO NEEDLE HERE MAY HOLD A COLON. The runner splits a directive on ':' to read
# its keys (record_parse.go; only expect=output and expect=stream are
# intercepted before that split), so `"old":[]` would be read as a key. Each
# needle below therefore starts or ends at a value boundary instead, which is
# why they look truncated: `[],"new"` is the empty Old immediately followed by
# the New field of the SAME record, and `["203.0.113.10"],"new"` is that
# address as the Old of the second one.
expect=file:path=firewall-domain-group.dns.jsonl:contains=[],"new"
expect=file:path=firewall-domain-group.dns.jsonl:contains=["203.0.113.10"]}
expect=file:path=firewall-domain-group.dns.jsonl:contains=["203.0.113.10"],"new"
expect=file:path=firewall-domain-group.dns.jsonl:contains=["203.0.113.11"]}
expect=file:path=firewall-domain-group.dns.jsonl:contains="web","name"
